WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.400
The COVID-19 coronavirus
is still spreading.

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:06.520
But while one early study found

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:09.640
that 80% of cases are considered mild,

00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:13.160
the definition of mild can
include a range of symptoms

00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:15.770
and still be painful, even though cases

00:00:15.770 --> 00:00:18.670
might not require hospitalization.

00:00:18.670 --> 00:00:20.840
Rishi Desai: I have mild
symptoms, I'm coughing.

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:22.930
But am I coughing once every minute,

00:00:22.930 --> 00:00:26.020
or is it every 10 minutes or five minutes?

00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:28.710
And if that starts to get
more and more frequent,

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:29.940
let's say I'm coughing every two minutes,

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:32.700
now every one minute, and
now I can't even speak to you

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:34.460
because I'm running out of breath,

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:36.520
those are the things that would trigger

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:37.587
me picking up the phone and saying,

00:00:37.587 --> 00:00:39.370
"Hey, you know what,
things are actually worse."

00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:41.370
Narrator: That's Dr. Rishi Desai,

00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:44.440
the chief medical officer at Osmosis.

00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:47.210
He says fatigue is the most common symptom

00:00:47.210 --> 00:00:48.850
of the coronavirus,

00:00:48.850 --> 00:00:51.490
but there are some other
ones to watch out for.

00:00:51.490 --> 00:00:53.640
Desai: So, typically, it slows you down.

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:56.040
It causes fatigue and malaise.

00:00:56.040 --> 00:01:00.090
You might be less inclined
to get up and do work.

00:01:00.090 --> 00:01:01.190
Maybe a sore throat,

00:01:01.190 --> 00:01:04.156
so maybe some focal symptoms like that.

00:01:04.156 --> 00:01:05.360
You can have a runny nose,

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:07.690
you can have some congestion in your nose.

00:01:07.690 --> 00:01:09.970
That inflammation in your
nose makes it hard to smell.

00:01:09.970 --> 00:01:12.150
It can also affect your
taste, so, all of a sudden,

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:14.270
things that you usually
would really enjoy,

00:01:14.270 --> 00:01:16.220
you're not really enjoying
those foods anymore.

00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:18.420
A lot of what I'm describing,
to be perfectly honest,

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:20.280
is a lot of the same
sort of general symptoms

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:23.550
or mild symptoms you get
with the common cold.

00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:25.480
Narrator: Likewise, the way to mitigate

00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:28.183
your mild coronavirus symptoms is largely

00:01:28.183 --> 00:01:30.750
the same way you'd
relieve your typical cold.

00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:34.390
Desai: For example, you
would drink a lot of fluids,

00:01:34.390 --> 00:01:36.189
you'd make sure those fluids
have a little bit of salt

00:01:36.189 --> 00:01:38.410
and a little bit of sugar
in them, because that way

00:01:38.410 --> 00:01:40.380
you get the fluid absorbed into your body.

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:43.620
So, a great example of that
would be broth or soup.

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:45.850
You'd made sure in general you're hydrated

00:01:45.850 --> 00:01:47.570
and that you're peeing
multiple times a day.

00:01:47.570 --> 00:01:49.500
You'd monitor your fever

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:51.362
and see if the fever is
getting better or worse.

00:01:51.362 --> 00:01:52.630
And if it's getting worse,

00:01:52.630 --> 00:01:54.510
maybe you take a medication like Tylenol.

00:01:54.510 --> 00:01:55.810
The one thing that's different

00:01:55.810 --> 00:01:57.343
that you would do with COVID

00:01:57.343 --> 00:01:59.550
is that you'd monitor your
symptoms really closely,

00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:01.640
because if they're getting worse,

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:04.595
then you'd want to call
and make sure you get seen.

00:02:04.595 --> 00:02:06.170
Narrator: The CDC says that

00:02:06.170 --> 00:02:07.890
if you're experiencing symptoms

00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:10.980
you think may be a result of COVID-19,

00:02:10.980 --> 00:02:14.420
seek medical attention by
calling your doctor first.

00:02:14.420 --> 00:02:16.330
If you have an underlying condition,

00:02:16.330 --> 00:02:18.680
like diabetes, asthma,

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:21.280
cardiovascular disease, or cancer,

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:24.070
and you think you might have
contracted the coronavirus,

00:02:24.070 --> 00:02:25.730
Desai says you should be seeking

00:02:25.730 --> 00:02:27.810
medical attention immediately.

00:02:27.810 --> 00:02:29.652
For those with mild symptoms,

00:02:29.652 --> 00:02:32.200
it's extremely important to monitor them.

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:34.770
To prevent the spread of coronavirus,

00:02:34.770 --> 00:02:37.280
do your best to limit contact with people.

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:39.590
Stay vigilant by washing your hands,

00:02:39.590 --> 00:02:41.840
cleaning the surfaces you use daily,

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:44.090
and monitoring your health.

